WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.231
This briefing is Book 13 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.231 --> 00:00:07.424
Governance, Drift and Evidence.

4
00:00:07.424 --> 00:00:10.949
It's about why compliance starts
to decay the day after the audit,

5
00:00:10.949 --> 00:00:13.675
and what governance that
doesn't decay looks like.

6
00:00:13.675 --> 00:00:15.050
We'll take four questions.

7
00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.725
Why does a signed package
start aging at once?

8
00:00:17.725 --> 00:00:20.388
What keeps governance
true between assessments?

9
00:00:20.388 --> 00:00:21.425
Who governs it?

10
00:00:21.425 --> 00:00:26.162
And how do you run it at machine tempo,
reuse it across agencies, and scale it?

11
00:00:26.162 --> 00:00:30.495
The short version: keep one authoritative
record, let the systems prove

12
00:00:30.495 --> 00:00:34.901
they still match it, and keep people
on the decisions only people can make.

13
00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:39.975
The slides and notes carry every detail
and source; I'll stick to what matters.

14
00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:42.800
Let's start with the day
the package is signed.

15
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.612
Picture the week an
authorization package is signed.

16
00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:48.824
On that day, the package is true.

17
00:00:48.824 --> 00:00:53.600
The next morning, an administrator
changes a firewall rule to fix an outage.

18
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.613
A contractor's account
outlives the contract.

19
00:00:56.613 --> 00:00:59.126
A cloud provider ships a new default.

20
00:00:59.126 --> 00:01:00.900
None of it reaches the package.

21
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.400
Federal guidance already
points away from the snapshot.

22
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.817
FISMA sets an annual testing floor,

23
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:11.400
and there's no federal three-year
rule; the term is the agency's to set.

24
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.699
NIST and OMB both push agencies
toward ongoing authorization,

25
00:01:15.699 --> 00:01:18.675
rather than a single
point-in-time decision.

26
00:01:18.675 --> 00:01:20.813
So why do so few systems get there?

27
00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:24.422
NIST answers that too: without
substantial automation,

28
00:01:24.422 --> 00:01:27.012
ongoing authorization isn't practical.

29
00:01:27.012 --> 00:01:29.442
And adding checkboxes makes the reporting

30
00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:31.938
heavier while leaving
the change untouched.

31
00:01:31.938 --> 00:01:33.838
That's the compliance paradox.

32
00:01:33.838 --> 00:01:36.460
Next, the name for what's going wrong.

33
00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.585
The mechanism has a name: drift.

34
00:01:39.585 --> 00:01:42.948
Drift is any difference between
what the authoritative record

35
00:01:42.948 --> 00:01:46.185
says should be true and what the
running estate actually does.

36
00:01:46.185 --> 00:01:48.448
A setting changed and never recorded.

37
00:01:48.448 --> 00:01:50.447
An account that outlived its owner.

38
00:01:50.447 --> 00:01:52.934
A rule that quietly stopped evaluating.

39
00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:56.867
NIST names the sources, from
attackers to honest mistakes,

40
00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:02.273
changes outside change control, provider
changes, staff turnover and upgrades.

41
00:02:02.273 --> 00:02:06.911
The program ranks drift first among
governance threats, for three reasons.

42
00:02:06.911 --> 00:02:10.222
It's continuous, a gradient
rather than an event.

43
00:02:10.222 --> 00:02:14.959
It's silent, because every console
reports what it was told, not what's true.

44
00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:17.405
And it's the opening other threats use;

45
00:02:17.405 --> 00:02:21.822
NSA and CISA call common
misconfigurations systemic weaknesses.

46
00:02:21.822 --> 00:02:26.169
NIST's controls already describe drift
detection without using the word:

47
00:02:26.169 --> 00:02:31.085
a baseline, controlled change, approved
deviations, and someone who keeps checking.

48
00:02:31.085 --> 00:02:33.980
Next, what drift is measured against.

49
00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.155
Drift can only be measured
against something.

50
00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:40.792
For twenty years, many
enterprises kept their governance,

51
00:02:40.792 --> 00:02:43.293
unwritten, in the Active Directory tree.

52
00:02:43.293 --> 00:02:45.654
A flat cloud directory doesn't carry it,

53
00:02:45.654 --> 00:02:50.006
so the program writes the governance
down, in one authoritative record.

54
00:02:50.006 --> 00:02:54.332
That record is a version-controlled
repository of policies,

55
00:02:54.332 --> 00:02:56.755
plans, evidence, schemas and rules.

56
00:02:56.755 --> 00:03:01.193
It doesn't master every fact;
HR still owns who a worker is.

57
00:03:01.193 --> 00:03:06.630
What it holds is the authority: which system
owns each fact, and who may write it.

58
00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.330
Runtime systems read
it and never write it.

59
00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:13.262
Private copies are forbidden,
because a local copy someone reads

60
00:03:13.262 --> 00:03:16.830
because the repository was slow
is a second source of truth.

61
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:22.043
And every derived report carries its
source, version, time and producer.

62
00:03:22.043 --> 00:03:27.180
In NIST terms, this record is the baseline,
kept in a form machines can compare.

63
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.720
Next, how changes get into it.

64
00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.445
A record is only as good
as the path into it.

65
00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:37.395
Most drift isn't malicious;
it's a hotfix during an outage.

66
00:03:37.395 --> 00:03:41.217
So the program allows one path
for every operational change,

67
00:03:41.217 --> 00:03:43.358
and no second path for urgent work.

68
00:03:43.358 --> 00:03:47.277
The path has five clauses:
originate from the source of truth,

69
00:03:47.277 --> 00:03:52.371
authorize from the authority model,
execute with just-in-time least privilege,

70
00:03:52.371 --> 00:03:56.446
close with verification, and
emit the evidence automatically.

71
00:03:56.446 --> 00:03:59.657
Skip a clause, and it's not
a change with a missing form.

72
00:03:59.657 --> 00:04:00.995
It's a drift finding.

73
00:04:00.995 --> 00:04:04.776
Changes to the record itself
travel as reviewed pull requests,

74
00:04:04.776 --> 00:04:08.557
with two people and protections
that bind administrators too.

75
00:04:08.557 --> 00:04:11.982
Emergencies are back-ported
within one business day,

76
00:04:11.982 --> 00:04:14.083
and reported as drift until then.

77
00:04:14.083 --> 00:04:15.358
Two rules sum it up.

78
00:04:15.358 --> 00:04:20.246
The identity that decides never acts,
and the one that acts never decides.

79
00:04:20.246 --> 00:04:23.207
And no closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

80
00:04:23.207 --> 00:04:25.200
Next, determinism.

81
00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.300
When the program says a control is
satisfied, how does anyone know it's right?

82
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:32.187
The usual answer is trust.

83
00:04:32.187 --> 00:04:34.575
The program's answer is determinism.

84
00:04:34.575 --> 00:04:38.182
Give it the same version of
the record and the same inputs,

85
00:04:38.182 --> 00:04:41.175
and it produces the same
evidence, byte for byte.

86
00:04:41.175 --> 00:04:43.675
So an assessor doesn't
have to trust the run.

87
00:04:43.675 --> 00:04:46.100
They repeat it and compare the hashes.

88
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.812
That's different from automation.

89
00:04:47.812 --> 00:04:50.711
Automation makes the answer arrive faster;

90
00:04:50.711 --> 00:04:54.413
determinism makes it the same
answer no matter who asks.

91
00:04:54.413 --> 00:04:58.012
The rules live in three layers,
each with its own reviewer:

92
00:04:58.012 --> 00:05:02.455
rules that say what things mean,
mappings that say which rules apply to

93
00:05:02.455 --> 00:05:06.974
which controls, and runtime code, the
only layer allowed to change state.

94
00:05:06.974 --> 00:05:11.988
One honest limit: determinism is faithful
to the record, not a check on it.

95
00:05:11.988 --> 00:05:15.175
A wrong rule gives the same
wrong answer every time.

96
00:05:15.175 --> 00:05:17.250
Next, the drift engine.

97
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.933
The drift engine compares what it
observes with the versioned record,

98
00:05:21.933 --> 00:05:23.900
not with a last-known-good snapshot.

99
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.788
An approved change updates the
record first, so it isn't drift.

100
00:05:27.788 --> 00:05:33.019
There's one drift model, with five classes,
each asking one question, like whether

101
00:05:33.019 --> 00:05:38.013
a value is still true, or whether every
change traces to an authorized source.

102
00:05:38.013 --> 00:05:42.824
The drift checks in the other books are
instances of these five, not separate lists.

103
00:05:42.824 --> 00:05:45.173
Severity is computed, not argued,

104
00:05:45.173 --> 00:05:48.913
from the class and whether
the claim is in active use.

105
00:05:48.913 --> 00:05:51.831
The top severity halts
and alerts a named role,

106
00:05:51.831 --> 00:05:56.287
and is never fixed automatically;
the others get set response windows.

107
00:05:56.287 --> 00:06:00.937
Severity sets the clock, and the
actuation ladder decides who may act.

108
00:06:00.937 --> 00:06:04.147
And be precise with an
assessor about what's built:

109
00:06:04.147 --> 00:06:08.062
two classes are built, one is
partial, and two are still design.

110
00:06:08.062 --> 00:06:10.980
Next, evidence versus attestation.

111
00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:16.405
An attestation is a person signing that
a system was in a stated condition.

112
00:06:16.405 --> 00:06:20.655
It answers who is accountable, and
federal authorization needs that answer.

113
00:06:20.655 --> 00:06:25.105
Evidence answers a different question:
is the claim true, and can I check it?

114
00:06:25.105 --> 00:06:27.692
The program inverts the usual package.

115
00:06:27.692 --> 00:06:32.484
Evidence is the substance, and
attestation is the accountability wrapper,

116
00:06:32.484 --> 00:06:36.967
so people sign for the rules, the
exceptions and the acceptance of risk.

117
00:06:36.967 --> 00:06:38.855
Federal guidance supports this.

118
00:06:38.855 --> 00:06:44.367
NIST treats automated comparison of
intended and actual state as equivalent to

119
00:06:44.367 --> 00:06:47.739
assessment results, and
FedRAMP's 2026 rules tell

120
00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:52.017
assessors not to simply review
documentation or screenshots.

121
00:06:52.017 --> 00:06:53.680
But be careful what you claim.

122
00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.505
A hash shows something changed.

123
00:06:55.505 --> 00:06:58.205
A hash chain shows removal or reordering.

124
00:06:58.205 --> 00:07:00.530
Only a signature shows who produced it.

125
00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:05.980
And some controls, like a training record
or a contract clause, stay attestations.

126
00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:09.200
Next, how the program
reaches each platform.

127
00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:13.613
The program doesn't replace
the platforms an agency runs.

128
00:07:13.613 --> 00:07:17.685
It reaches each one through an
adapter: a translator that reads the

129
00:07:17.685 --> 00:07:22.424
platform's native state and writes it
into the common record, with provenance.

130
00:07:22.424 --> 00:07:26.451
The rules, the drift engine and
the evidence chain see one record,

131
00:07:26.451 --> 00:07:28.350
whichever vendor produced it.

132
00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.662
And adapters hold no rules
and make no decisions.

133
00:07:31.662 --> 00:07:34.612
There are two kinds,
kept in separate lists.

134
00:07:34.612 --> 00:07:36.887
Conformance adapters are read-only.

135
00:07:36.887 --> 00:07:39.455
Modernization adapters change state,

136
00:07:39.455 --> 00:07:43.226
only on authorized plans, and
with an approver by default.

137
00:07:43.226 --> 00:07:46.400
Read-only comes first,
and it has to be verified.

138
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.478
ScubaGear is read-only in purpose,

139
00:07:48.478 --> 00:07:52.411
yet a Global Administrator always
has the permissions it needs,

140
00:07:52.411 --> 00:07:56.937
so the program compares what an identity
is granted with what it declares.

141
00:07:56.937 --> 00:07:58.775
Lock-in doesn't disappear.

142
00:07:58.775 --> 00:08:03.850
It moves to small, replaceable adapters,
and to a record the agency owns.

143
00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.810
Next, who decides.

144
00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:08.948
None of this machinery decides anything.

145
00:08:08.948 --> 00:08:12.306
A drift finding asks a
question: change the estate,

146
00:08:12.306 --> 00:08:15.173
change the record, or
carry the difference?

147
00:08:15.173 --> 00:08:19.935
Only people can answer the last two, so
the program names one decider for each.

148
00:08:19.935 --> 00:08:23.529
The steward keeps the record,
its protections and backups,

149
00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:27.273
but doesn't decide what the facts
mean; the business owners do.

150
00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:31.747
The CISO decides control requirements,
even when they're written as code.

151
00:08:31.747 --> 00:08:35.582
The governance board is the change
control body NIST calls for,

152
00:08:35.582 --> 00:08:40.448
and it reviews rather than votes, so every
decision keeps one accountable owner.

153
00:08:40.448 --> 00:08:44.239
The authorizing official, the
only one who can accept risk,

154
00:08:44.239 --> 00:08:49.373
signs every open-ended gap and every
class of change allowed to run unattended.

155
00:08:49.373 --> 00:08:55.234
A bounded exception, with an end date, is
the CISO's, and none renews automatically.

156
00:08:55.234 --> 00:08:58.078
And because the repository
is a federal record,

157
00:08:58.078 --> 00:09:01.722
its history is never rewritten
without the records officer.

158
00:09:01.722 --> 00:09:03.350
Next, tempo.

159
00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.471
A platform that detects
perfectly but closes slowly

160
00:09:07.471 --> 00:09:10.613
produces an accurate list
of things nobody fixed.

161
00:09:10.613 --> 00:09:12.337
Tempo keeps that list short.

162
00:09:12.337 --> 00:09:17.137
The program sets a response window for
each severity, and external clocks win,

163
00:09:17.137 --> 00:09:20.962
like the vulnerability directive
or CISA's incident notice.

164
00:09:20.962 --> 00:09:23.813
The usual ticket sends
a person to a console,

165
00:09:23.813 --> 00:09:26.663
and their reply of done
is just an assertion.

166
00:09:26.663 --> 00:09:30.771
The program's rule is that wherever
a machine can reach the surface,

167
00:09:30.771 --> 00:09:32.788
the ticket authorizes a machine.

168
00:09:32.788 --> 00:09:37.477
A person approves, and the platform
executes from a versioned plan,

169
00:09:37.477 --> 00:09:40.888
after a dry run, then re-reads
the object to verify.

170
00:09:40.888 --> 00:09:44.612
By default, a named approver
releases each instance.

171
00:09:44.612 --> 00:09:49.589
Only classes the authorizing official
accepts in advance run on their own,

172
00:09:49.589 --> 00:09:52.399
and some, like Conditional
Access, never do.

173
00:09:52.399 --> 00:09:55.786
Where no machine can reach,
the fallback is a declared,

174
00:09:55.786 --> 00:09:59.788
recorded privileged session,
never a quiet trip to the console.

175
00:09:59.788 --> 00:10:01.850
Next, reciprocity.

176
00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:07.219
A platform that twenty agencies use gets
examined by twenty assessment teams,

177
00:10:07.219 --> 00:10:10.488
producing twenty snapshots
at twenty different times.

178
00:10:10.488 --> 00:10:15.376
Reciprocity is the alternative: agencies
accept each other's assessments.

179
00:10:15.376 --> 00:10:20.213
But nothing in it lets one authorizing
official accept risk for another agency.

180
00:10:20.213 --> 00:10:23.956
NIST gives mechanisms for this,
like an authorization to use,

181
00:10:23.956 --> 00:10:29.047
where an agency accepts another's package
and carries full responsibility for it,

182
00:10:29.047 --> 00:10:34.288
or a joint authorization, where several
officials accept one system's risk together.

183
00:10:34.288 --> 00:10:39.357
For cloud services, OMB makes reuse
the default: agencies presume a FedRAMP

184
00:10:39.357 --> 00:10:44.263
package is adequate, but only while
continuous monitoring maintains it.

185
00:10:44.263 --> 00:10:47.437
And each agency still
authorizes its own systems.

186
00:10:47.437 --> 00:10:51.101
So the program corrects its old
single-authorization wording.

187
00:10:51.101 --> 00:10:56.712
It's one package, and many decisions, with
each agency signing for its own risk.

188
00:10:56.712 --> 00:10:58.340
Next, scale.

189
00:10:59.090 --> 00:11:01.590
A real agency doesn't have one estate.

190
00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.688
It has hundreds of systems
and several boundaries,

191
00:11:04.688 --> 00:11:09.852
and NIST expects each system's monitoring
to follow one organization-wide strategy.

192
00:11:09.852 --> 00:11:12.300
The program's scaling rule is simple:

193
00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.565
hold the definitions small,
and let the uses grow.

194
00:11:15.565 --> 00:11:19.978
The record's core, the drift classes
and the severities stay fixed,

195
00:11:19.978 --> 00:11:24.227
so a top-severity finding means
the same thing in every program.

196
00:11:24.227 --> 00:11:28.653
Adapters, mappings and evidence
grow, each validated on entry.

197
00:11:28.653 --> 00:11:31.368
Boundaries are recorded
as governed objects,

198
00:11:31.368 --> 00:11:33.940
so findings cite the
boundary they crossed.

199
00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.078
And coverage is reported honestly.

200
00:11:36.078 --> 00:11:40.428
Every control is covered,
partial, a gap, or out of scope.

201
00:11:40.428 --> 00:11:43.897
Every partial or gap goes into
a register with an owner,

202
00:11:43.897 --> 00:11:46.903
the evidence that will
prove closure, and a date.

203
00:11:46.903 --> 00:11:48.789
Closed items stay for audit.

204
00:11:48.789 --> 00:11:50.990
Let's close with the decisions.

205
00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:53.115
So, four decisions.

206
00:11:53.115 --> 00:11:56.676
Charter the governance board in
writing and name the steward,

207
00:11:56.676 --> 00:11:59.190
with one decider for
every kind of decision.

208
00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:04.530
Settle the record before the next migration:
which system owns each fact, the rules,

209
00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.042
and the provenance each value carries,

210
00:12:07.042 --> 00:12:10.890
so a migration changes who supplies
a fact, not what it means.

211
00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:15.201
Set the tempo: response windows by
severity, an approver by default,

212
00:12:15.201 --> 00:12:20.140
and the authorizing official's signature
on every class that runs on its own.

213
00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:22.622
And plan the move to
ongoing authorization,

214
00:12:22.622 --> 00:12:26.965
on evidence the systems produce rather
than attestations people assemble.

215
00:12:26.965 --> 00:12:29.165
The next step needs no procurement.

216
00:12:29.165 --> 00:12:32.990
Classify every control's
coverage, open the gap register,

217
00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:37.628
and record every drift finding in
the same shape, however it was found.

218
00:12:37.628 --> 00:12:39.503
A package describes one day.

219
00:12:39.503 --> 00:12:43.902
Governance that doesn't decay keeps
proving itself every day after.

220
00:12:43.902 --> 00:12:47.614
Book 13 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

221
00:12:47.614 --> 00:12:48.430
Thank you.
